# SYNOPSIS

```swift
public enum Note {
  public static var max: Int
  public static func post(_ text: String, to process: borrowing Handle)
    throws(Status)
  public static func diedOf(_ process: borrowing Handle) -> String?
  public static func notify(
    _ handler: @escaping @Sendable (String) -> Bool) throws(Status)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A note is a short text posted to a process, as in Plan 9: `interrupt` when the terminal's interrupt key is typed, `hangup` when its connection goes, `kill`.
Notes are posted through proc(4), whose service holds the process handles; a program writes to `/proc/<pid>/note` and rarely calls `post` itself.

A process that hasn't called `notify` dies of the first note posted to it, with the note as its exit string.
One that has gets each note in its handler, oldest first, on a thread `notify` starts; if the handler returns false, the process ends with that note as its exit string, as it would have without one.

: `max`
  The longest a note may be, 127 bytes; a note has at least one byte and no NUL.
: `post`
  Posts <text> to <process>.
  The handle needs the right to post notes, which a process's creator's handle has.
  It throws `shouldWait` when five notes already wait, Plan 9's limit, and `badState` when the process isn't running.
: `diedOf`
  The note <process> died of, once it has ended; nil if it ended any other way.
: `notify`
  From now on this process takes its notes, each given to <handler>.
  A process calls it once.

# EXAMPLES

rc keeps the interrupt for its loop to see, and lets its foreground program die of it:

```swift
try Note.notify { note in
  pending.append(note)
  return true
}
```

# SEE ALSO

proc(4), rc(1), intro(2)

# BUGS

A call the process is blocked in isn't interrupted: its handler runs beside it.
